# PATENT SPECIFICATION

## TITLE OF INVENTION
**MICRO-NEEDLE ARRAY DEVICE FOR TRANSDERMAL PENETRATION WITH CONTROLLED DEPTH DELIVERY TO THE DERMIS**

---

## FIELD OF THE INVENTION

The present invention relates generally to devices for transdermal drug delivery and biological sampling, and more particularly to a micro-needle array structure configured to reliably penetrate the stratum corneum and epidermal layers of skin while ensuring consistent access to the dermal layer, thereby enabling effective substance delivery or fluid extraction without causing pain receptor stimulation associated with deeper penetration.

---

## BACKGROUND OF THE INVENTION

Conventional transdermal delivery methods, including topical creams and hypodermic injections, present limitations in either penetration efficacy or patient comfort. Topical applications often fail to breach the stratum corneum, the primary barrier to transdermal absorption, while hypodermic needles penetrate deeply into nerve-rich tissue, causing pain and requiring trained administration. There exists a need for a micro-needle structure engineered with precise geometric and material characteristics to consistently achieve penetration depths sufficient to reach the dermis while remaining shallow enough to minimize discomfort and enable self-administration.

---

## SUMMARY OF THE INVENTION

The present invention provides a micro-needle array device comprising a plurality of individual micro-needles affixed to or integrally formed with a base substrate. Each micro-needle is dimensioned and shaped to penetrate the epidermal layer, including the stratum corneum, and terminate within the dermal layer, thereby facilitating controlled release of therapeutic agents, vaccines, or biological markers, or alternatively, enabling extraction of interstitial fluid for diagnostic purposes.

Two preferred embodiments are disclosed herein: a **solid micro-needle embodiment** configured for coated or dissolvable substance delivery, and a **hollow micro-needle embodiment** configured for fluid infusion or aspiration.

---

## DETAILED DESCRIPTION OF THE PREFERRED EMBODIMENTS

### First Embodiment — Solid Micro-Needle Array Structure

Referring to the accompanying drawings, the first embodiment of the micro-needle device (**100**) comprises the following numbered components:

- **(101)** — A base substrate, formed from a biocompatible polymer or metal, providing structural support for the micro-needle array;
- **(102)** — A plurality of solid micro-needles extending outwardly from the base substrate (101), each having a proximal end integrally affixed to said substrate;
- **(103)** — A tapered shaft portion of each micro-needle (102), configured with a diameter sufficient to maintain mechanical rigidity during insertion;
- **(104)** — A distal tip portion of reduced cross-sectional geometry relative to shaft (103), configured to minimize insertion force and facilitate penetration through the stratum corneum (105a) and viable epidermis (105b);
- **(105)** — The epidermal layer of the skin, through which the micro-needle (102) is designed to pass;
- **(106)** — The dermal layer, representing the target termination zone of the distal tip (104), wherein said dermal layer (106) contains vasculature and interstitial fluid suitable for substance absorption or extraction;
- **(107)** — A coating layer disposed upon the outer surface of shaft (103) and tip (104), comprising a therapeutic agent configured to dissolve or elute upon contact with interstitial fluid present in the dermis (106);
- **(108)** — A depth-limiting collar or shoulder feature disposed at the base of each micro-needle (102), configured to abut the outer surface of the epidermis (105) upon full insertion, thereby preventing over-penetration into subcutaneous tissue (109).

In operation, the base substrate (101) is applied to the skin surface with sufficient force to drive the plurality of micro-needles (102) through the epidermis (105), whereupon the depth-limiting collar (108) contacts the skin surface, ensuring that the distal tip (104) reaches, but does not substantially exceed, the dermal layer (106). The coating (107) subsequently dissolves, releasing the therapeutic agent into the dermis (106).

---

### Second Embodiment — Hollow Micro-Needle Array Structure with Fluid Channel

Referring further to the drawings, a second embodiment of the micro-needle device (**200**) comprises the following numbered components:

- **(201)** — A base substrate structurally analogous to component (101), further comprising a fluid reservoir or manifold integrated therein;
- **(202)** — A plurality of hollow micro-needles extending from base substrate (201), each defining an internal lumen (203);
- **(203)** — An internal lumen extending longitudinally through each micro-needle (202), fluidly coupled to the reservoir within base substrate (201);
- **(204)** — A beveled distal tip disposed at the terminus of each hollow micro-needle (202), configured to facilitate penetration through the epidermis (205) and to provide an open aperture communicating with lumen (203);
- **(205)** — The epidermal layer, corresponding functionally to layer (105) of the first embodiment;
- **(206)** — The dermal layer, corresponding functionally to layer (106), representing the intended termination depth of tip (204);
- **(207)** — A penetration depth of between approximately 300 micrometers and 1500 micrometers, measured from the base substrate (201) to the distal tip (204), selected to ensure consistent access to dermal layer (206) across varying skin thicknesses while avoiding contact with subcutaneous nerve fibers;
- **(208)** — A fluid control mechanism, in fluid communication with the reservoir of base substrate (201), configured to regulate the rate of fluid delivery through lumen (203), or alternatively, to apply negative pressure for extraction of interstitial fluid from the dermis (206) into the reservoir;
- **(209)** — A depth-limiting shoulder feature, functionally analogous to component (108), disposed circumferentially about the base of each hollow micro-needle (202).

In operation, the fluid control mechanism (208) actuates to drive a therapeutic fluid through the reservoir of base substrate (201), through lumen (203), and out through the aperture of distal tip (204), delivering said fluid directly into the dermal layer (206). Alternatively, the fluid control mechanism (208) may be actuated in reverse to withdraw interstitial fluid from the dermis (206) for diagnostic analysis.

---

## ADVANTAGES OF THE INVENTION

The micro-needle structures disclosed in both embodiments (100) and (200) provide the following advantages:

1. Consistent and repeatable penetration depth through the epidermis (105/205) to the dermis (106/206), regardless of manual application variability, due to the depth-limiting features (108/209);
2. Minimization of pain sensation, as penetration depth (207) is calibrated to avoid subcutaneous nerve tissue (109);
3. Adaptability to either solid coated delivery (first embodiment) or active fluid infusion/extraction (second embodiment);
4. Suitability for self-administration by untrained users due to reduced insertion force requirements enabled by tip geometries (104/204).

---

